  "query_preview": "(This evening through Wednesday)...\nIssued at 254 PM EDT Wed Sep 2 2026\n\nStrong mid/upper-level ridging over the CONUS remains in place \nthrough the end of the week, amplifying over the Plains this \nweekend. With the main ridge axis amplifying to our west, troughing \nover the northeastern US may nudge southwestward into Indiana late \nSaturday or Sunday. This could allow for a backdoor cold front out \nof the northeast, bringing an end to the current heat wave. The \nreprieve may not last long as ensemble guidance hints at the ridge \nbuilding back eastward next week with above-average temperatures \nreturning to the region.\n\nIn the meantime, heat and humidity continue to build over the Ohio \nRiver Valley. Temperatures have risen into the mid 90s with dew \npoints in the low 70s. ACARS soundings out of IND show around 1500 \nJ/Kg MLCAPE with guidance showing an increase to around 2500 J/Kg \nlater this afternoon. The IND soundings also show a fairly deep but \nmodest cap around 700mb. The profile overall is rather dry as well. \nNevertheless, CAM guidance hints at some isolated convective \ndevelopment later today into the early evening. Forcing seems \nnebulous at best, and initiation will likely depend on subtle \nfeatures such as horizontal convective rolls. Severe potential \nappears low, but with such a dry profile amid relatively steep lapse \nrates, a downburst capable of strong winds cannot be ruled out. \nOverall, chances of any one location experiencing convection today \nis low and severe convection even lower.\n\nThursday is similar to today with heat, humidity, and \nafternoon/evening storm chances. Thunderstorm potential looks to be \na bit better than today due to influence from upstream convection \n(remnant outflow). The greatest potential exists across our northern \nCWA, and especially into northern Indiana. Depending on how upstream \nconvection evolves overnight which determines the position of the \noutflow boundary, subsequent development may not even reach central \nIndiana. Isolated convection is also possible further south but \nchances are low. Severe potential exists in the form of strong wind \ngusts due to steep low-level lapse rates and high DCAPE (over 1000 \nK/Kg). Like today, overall chances are still low and severe \npotential even lower.\n\nBy Friday, the amplifying ridge to our west will allow upper flow to \nbecome more northerly. Guidance shows northeasterly surface flow \nover Ohio pushing a thermal gradient southwestward towards Indiana. \nUpstream convection may tend to ride along this gradient within the \nnortherly flow aloft. Low-level flow over Indiana is out of the west-\nsouthwest at the time, which may encourage some west/southwestward \npropagation. Like with any potential late summer MCS, strong winds \nand flooding are the primary hazards. Activity may linger into \nSaturday morning, depending on how far upstream and how late it \nforms.\n\nThe thermal gradient then advances southwestward as a backdoor cold \nfront on Saturday allowing temperatures to return to near average \nthis weekend, perhaps even a bit below average.",
